    "lawCode": "Amd §14, Transp L",
    "lawSection": "Transportation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8057\n\nSPONSOR: RITCHIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the transportation law, in relation to establishing a\ncommercial driver's license (CDL) class A young adult licensing pilot\nprogram\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill will create a pilot program to allow young drivers 18 to 20\nyears of age to operate commercial vehicles within intrastate commerce,\nas is allowed in 48 other states.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds new subdivision 36 to section 14 of the transportation\nlaw, directing the NYS Commissioner of Transportation to establish the\nCDL class A young adult licensing program, in consultation with NYS\nDepartment of Motor Vehicles. Drivers between the ages of 18 and 21 will\nbe able to receive a class A CDL to operate commercial vehicles within\nintrastate commerce, within the restrictions established by this subdi-\nvision and those set by the commissioner of transportation.\n\nSection 2 provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York's Commercial Drivers are the backbone of the state's economy,\nwith the trucking industry responsible for more than 275,000 jobs, or 1\nout of 27 in the state, paying wages in excess of $13.6 billion annual-\nly, according to the American Transportation Research Institute.\n\nUnfortunately, New York's trucking industry is finding it increasingly\ndifficult to recruit commercial drivers to supply the state's 38,580\ntrucking companies, most of them small, locally owned businesses.\n\nAn analysis commissioned by the American Trucking Association concluded\nthat as of the end of 2017 the industry had a 50,000-driver shortage.\nWith the industry estimating it needs to hire 890,000 drivers over the\nnext 10 years.\n\nThe average of age of a private carrier driver is 52 and according to\nthe Bureau of Labor statistics only 6% of drivers are women. The indus-\ntry needs young people and women to offset the driver shortage.\n\nMeanwhile, with companies facing increasing costs for insurance, workers\ncompensation, and safety issues, the industry is insisting on stricter\nstandards to improve the quality of the drivers on the road.\n\nCurrently 48 states allow 18 to 21 year olds to drive Class 8 trucks\nwithin state lines. A young adult commercial driver's license pilot\nprogram that would allow 18 to 20-year-olds to drive in New York could\nhelp attract more young people into the field.\n\nGranting graduates of the pilot program Class A commercial driver's\nlicenses with the stipulation that they cannot drive after 9 p.m.  would\nhelp more people enter the field at a critical time in their lives when\nmany are choosing their profession. Moreover, by having the experience\nof driving Class A trucks at a younger age the ability to reach higher\nwage levels will be expedited.\n\nThe commissioner would have an opportunity to report to the legislature\nand the governor in 2021 on the results of the pilot program. The report\nwould show how many became fully licensed at 21 and whether the pilot\nproject revealed any safety concerns, including numbers of accidents and\ntraffic violations.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
